;; Endeavor 146 — a REGISTER OPERAND tile stored straight out, differentiated.  No MMA at all.
;;
;; WHY THIS SPEC EXISTS.  Chris found the defect by hand, running 142/13 in a configuration
;; that spec does not declare (it is a PTX negative test; he tried --ir-target=spv):
;;
;;     Unsupported form '%LOAD-REGISTER-TILE-ACC' found in function body.
;;
;; Nothing in the suite covered it, because 142/13 is skipped under --differentiate (correctly
;; — it is a negative test) and every other register-tile spec routes through an MMA.  So the
;; simplest possible register-operand kernel had no differentiate coverage anywhere.  It does
;; now.
;;
;; THE DEFECT was a half-applied change of my own.  Gap 4 split the two register-tile ROLES
;; apart at the adjoint:
;;   - ACCUMULATOR  -> adjoint is a register tile (seeded by %load-register-tile-acc)
;;   - OPERAND      -> adjoint is a SCRATCH MATRIX, because every consumer indexes it as
;;                     memory and a register tile cannot be written element-wise
;; but the store-tile backward still asked the BROAD question, %mma-ad-register-tile-p, and so
;; emitted %load-register-tile-acc — a REGISTER operation — against a scratch-matrix adjoint.
;; The guard now asks %mma-ad-register-accumulator-tile-p, and an operand tile falls through to
;; the ordinary store-tile-at backward, which is the memory-shaped edge its adjoint wants:
;;
;;     (%STORE-TILE-AT-BWD A-TILE_ADJ C_GRAD ...)   ; gather C_GRAD -> tile adjoint
;;     (%LOAD-TILE-AT-BWD  A_GRAD A-TILE_ADJ ...)   ; scatter tile adjoint -> A_GRAD
;;
;; a clean reverse of the forward's A -> tile -> C.
;;
;; NO NUMERIC CHECK, AND THE REASON IS WORTH RECORDING — it is a finding, not an omission.
;;
;; The obvious check would be f(A) = sum(C) with C = A elementwise, giving dA[i,j] = 1.0.  That
;; was written and RUN, and it read:
;;
;;     analytical=1.0   numerical=0.0
;;
;; The ANALYTICAL side is correct.  The finite difference is what disagrees: perturbing A[0,0]
;; does not change sum(C) on metal, so the FORWARD does not compute "C = A elementwise".  An
;; `:operand :a` tile holds its data in the MMA's GRF FRAGMENT layout, not row-major, so
;; "load it and store it back" is not an identity — that was the test's premise, not the
;; language's.  (A pure permutation would still give FD = 1.0 for sum(C), so a zero suggests
;; the store writes nothing reachable at all.  Whether storing an :operand tile is meant to be
;; expressible is an open FORWARD question, unrelated to AD, and no spec covers it — 142/13,
;; the only kernel of this shape, is a compile-only PTX negative test.)
;;
;; So this spec asserts what it can honestly prove: the kernel COMPILES under --differentiate
;; and the backward is the memory-shaped pair above.  The gradient VALUES for register operand
;; tiles are proven where the forward is metal-verified instead: 142/01 (MMA_CORRECT on BMG)
;; reads analytical=1.2, and 145/12 the same.
;;
;; COMPILE-WITH[--hardware-profile=bmg --ir-target=spv]: PASS
;; COMPILE-WITH[--hardware-profile=bmg --ir-target=spv --differentiate]: PASS
;; SKIP-DEFAULT-PASS

(def-hardware-profile bmg :simd-width 16 :mma-shapes ((8 16 8)))

(def-type mat (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

(def-kernel register_operand_store (A &out C)
  (declare #'(mat &out mat)
           (global-size :set-to 16)
           (local-size  :set-to 16))
  (let ((A-tile (make-register-tile float (16 8) 0.0 :operand :a)))
    (load-tile A A-tile (0 0))          ; global -> GRF, the Phase A register overload
    (store-tile A-tile C (0 0))))       ; GRF -> global, no MMA in between
